Android: no Render to RAM on older Adreno drivers (warecomp).

A Moto G60 (Adreno 618, driver 0x801f6000, Android 12) crashed in the driver (reading
0xf0) while the renderer copied a rendered framebuffer back into the console's memory at a
full sync (State::fullSync, NativeTarget::copyToNative). Another N64 port's Android build hit
the same on that phone and an Adreno 630, and avoided it by turning that copy off. Worms
Armageddon then shows its boot screens later (the publisher logo at about 12 s instead of
2.5 s, checked on desktop) but the title, menus and matches draw the same. On Android,
Qualcomm drivers older than 0x80212000 (an Adreno 619 on Android 15 runs it fine) start with
Render to RAM off.

--- a/src/hle/rt64_application.cpp
+++ b/src/hle/rt64_application.cpp
@@ -231,6 +231,19 @@
         fprintf(stdout, "Device Vendor: 0x%X\n", deviceDescription.vendor);
         fprintf(stdout, "Driver Version: 0x%" PRIx64 "\n", deviceDescription.driverVersion);
 
+#   if defined(__ANDROID__)
+        // warecomp: older Qualcomm Adreno drivers (a Moto G60's Adreno 618, driver 0x801f6000,
+        // Android 12) crash inside the driver while the renderer copies a rendered framebuffer back
+        // into the console's memory at a full sync (another N64 port found the same on that phone
+        // and an Adreno 630). Without that copy (Render to RAM off) Worms Armageddon draws its
+        // boot screens later but otherwise the same. Drivers older than 0x80212000 (an Adreno 619
+        // on Android 15, which runs it fine) turn it off.
+        if ((uint32_t(deviceDescription.vendor) == 0x5143) && (deviceDescription.driverVersion < 0x80212000)) {
+            emulatorConfig.framebuffer.renderToRAM = false;
+            fprintf(stdout, "[RT64] Older Adreno driver: rendered frames aren't copied back to the console's memory.\n");
+        }
+#   endif
+
         // Detect if the application should use hardware resolve or not.
         bool usesHardwareResolve = (userConfig.hardwareResolve != UserConfiguration::HardwareResolve::Disabled);
 
